 **`focus()` on a window that lives on another desktop switches to that desktop first**, so focus never lands on a window the user cannot see. `HOOKS.DESKTOP_SWITCHED` fires before the window's own `WINDOW_FOCUSED`. `open()` never does this: a window opened onto an inactive desktop (session restore, a lazily loaded native window) joins it without taking focus, and the active desktop stays where it is.
 
+**Keep on top.** `setAlwaysOnTop( id, true )` keeps a window painted above every window that is not kept on top, including one focused or opened later; the user does the same with the pin button (`core/keep-on-top`) in the title bar. It changes stacking only, never focus: the focused window can sit underneath a kept one, and `getFocused()` still answers with it. Several windows can be kept on top at once, and among themselves they stack in focus order. An open child of a kept window is kept with it, so ownership still holds. `setAlwaysOnTop( id, false )` puts the window back into normal stacking. Read the state from `win.alwaysOnTop`. A change dispatches `os-window-changed` with `reason: 'always-on-top'`, and the flag is part of the window's session entry, so it survives a reload. Fullscreen still covers everything.
+
 #### Child windows — Stable
 
 A **child window** is a real window — own chrome, drag, resize, minimize, taskbar entry — with one rule layered on top: **its owner can never sit above it.** Clicking the owner hands focus to the child and shakes it rather than raising the owner.
